section.center(data-tag="keep", data-state="hide-menubar")
	h1 Tagteam
	h3 for Reveal.js
	hr
	code.url#exampleParameter .../demo.html?t=tagteam

section(data-name="What is it", data-tag="intro")

	section.center
		h2 What is it?

	section.center
		p Tagteam is used to dynamically show only certain sections in presentations when you don’t want to show everything. It uses URL parameters to do that.

		p.small The bar along the top is #[a(href="https://github.com/martinomagnifico/reveal.js-simplemenu", target="_blank") Simplemenu], loaded here on purpose: it is the quickest way to see a chapter added or removed.

	section.center
		p With Tagteam you can: 
		ul
			li Show only sections with certain tags
			li Show only sections with certain names
			li Show predefined groups of sections
			li Hide a set of sections until the URL asks for them
			li Reveal a hidden section without touching the rest
			li Combine these methods for flexible control

	section.center
		p Tagteam reads 3 different URL parameters:
		ol
			li #[code ?t=abcd] for #[code data-tag] sections
			li #[code ?n=abcd] for #[code data-name] sections
			li #[code ?g=abcd] for predefined groups

	section.center
		p Tagteam only looks at the sections you have marked. One with neither a #[code data-tag] nor a #[code data-name] is left alone.
		p.small When a parameter filters, the marked sections it does not name are hidden. With no parameter at all nothing is filtered, so the deck you wrote is the deck you get.

	section.center
		p Sections you list in #[code hide] work the other way round.
		p.small An ordinary tagged section is already hidden by a parameter that does not name it. #[code hide] changes two things: it starts hidden even with #[em no] parameter at all, and naming it does not filter the rest of the deck the way naming an ordinary tag would.
		p.small In #[code "unhide"] mode neither is special. Nothing filters there to begin with, so this is simply how the mode works.

	section.center
		h4 Two ways to use it
		//- Row and column headers are marked up as such, so that a screen reader
		//- reading one cell can say which row it is in. Without that, the second
		//- column is announced on its own and means nothing.
		table.threeways
			thead
				tr
					th(scope="col") #[code mode]
					th(scope="col") What a URL parameter does
			tbody
				tr
					th(scope="row") #[code "select"]
					td Picks what is shown, and hides every other marked section
				tr
					th(scope="row") #[code "unhide"]
					td Shows what it names, and leaves everything else alone
		p.small #[code "select"] is the default. With no parameter at all neither mode filters anything, so the deck is what you wrote, less whatever it holds out.

	section.center
		h4 Always shown
		p.small A section with #[code data-tag="keep"], like the title slide of this presentation, is left out of both filters and is always shown, except when a group is mandatory.
		p.small It only matters in #[code "select"] mode, and only for a section that also has a #[code data-tag] or a #[code data-name]. An unmarked section is already always shown, and in #[code "unhide"] mode no parameter ever hides an ordinary marked one.
		p.small #[code keep] has to be the whole attribute value. #[code data-tag="keep, intro"] is an ordinary tagged section, and is filtered like any other.



section(data-name="Setup", data-tag="intro")

	section.center
		h2 Setup

	section
		p Set up your HTML with data-tags and/or data-names:
		pre
			code.hljs.html(data-trim, data-line-numbers).
				&lt;section data-name="Brown" data-tag="nice"&gt;
				    &lt;section data-tag="cats, small"&gt;Cat (brown, small, nice)&lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-tag="dogs"&gt;Dog (brown, nice)&lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-tag="dogs, big"&gt;Dog (brown, big, nice)&lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-tag="horses"&gt;Horse (brown, nice)&lt;/section&gt;
				&lt;/section&gt;
				&lt;section data-name="Black &amp;amp; white"&gt;
				    &lt;section data-tag="cats"&gt;Cat (black &amp;amp; white)&lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-tag="dogs, small"&gt;Dog (black &amp;amp; white, small)&lt;/section&gt;
				    &lt;section data-tag="horses"&gt;Horse (black &amp;amp; white)&lt;/section&gt;
				&lt;/section&gt;
		//-
		p.small The above markup is used in all following examples.
		p.small Note: #[code data-tag] can hold several comma-separated tags. #[code data-name] holds a single name, which may be several words long.

	section
		h4 JavaScript
		p.small Load the script and make a reference to it in the Reveal plugin options.
		pre
			code.hljs.html(data-line-numbers).
				&lt;script src="plugin/tagteam/tagteam.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
				&lt;script&gt;
				    Reveal.initialize({
				        plugins: [ Tagteam ]
				    });
				&lt;/script&gt;
		//-
		p.small When using the Markdown plugin as well, load it first, so Tagteam can find the tagged or named sections it writes.

	section
		h4 Choose a mode
		p.small #[code mode] says how the deck behaves. This demo does not set it, so Tags, Names and Groups below all run on the default, #[code "select"]: a parameter picks what is shown and hides every other marked section.
		pre
			code.hljs.js(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
				    //...
				    tagteam: {
				        mode: "select"
				    },
				    plugins: [ Tagteam ]
				});
		//-
		p.small #[code "unhide"] is the other mode: nothing is hidden by a parameter, only shown. See the #[a(href="demo-select.html") select demo] and the #[a(href="demo-unhide.html") unhide demo] for a full deck built around each.

section(data-name="Tags", data-tag="intro")

	section.center
		h2 Tags

	section.center
		h4 Using a single #[code t] parameter
		p.small #[code.url .../demo.html?t=dogs]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="?t=dogs") demo.html?t=dogs]

	section.center
		h4 Using multiple #[code t] parameters
		p.small #[code.url .../demo.html?t=dogs,cats]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="?t=dogs,cats") demo.html?t=dogs,cats]

	section.center
		h4 Using more specific #[code t] parameters
		p.small Use the plus sign to be more specific.#[br]In this case we want all dogs, but only small cats.
		p.small #[code.url .../demo.html?t=dogs,cats+small]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="?t=dogs,cats+small") demo.html?t=dogs,cats+small]

	section.center
		h4 Using more specific #[code t] parameters
		p.small This works with inherited tags as well: a vertical slide also counts the tags on its stack.
		p.small #[code.url .../demo.html?t=small+cats,nice+horses]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="?t=small+cats,nice+horses") demo.html?t=small+cats,nice+horses]

	section.center
		h4 Tags work the same in both modes
		p.small Here, in #[code "select"], #[code ?t=dogs] shows the dogs and hides everything else that is tagged.
		p.small In #[code "unhide"], the same #[code ?t=dogs] would only reveal a #[code dogs] section that #[code hide] had marked. Nothing else would move.

section(data-name="Names", data-tag="intro")

	section.center
		h2 Names
		p When ordering your presentation with nested sections, you can specify these to be shown in one fell swoop.

	section.center
		h4 Using an #[code n] parameter
		p.small There are two named stacks with vertical slides. Now we only want the first (#[code data-name="Brown"]) stack.
		p.small #[code.url .../demo.html?n=brown]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="?n=brown") demo.html?n=brown]

	section.center
		h4 Using multiple #[code n] parameters
		p.small #[code.url .../demo.html?n=brown,black+%26+white]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="?n=brown,black+%26+white") demo.html?n=brown,black+%26+white]

	section.center
		h4 Combining names and tags
		p.small Only black &amp; white horses, and small black &amp; white dogs.
		p.small #[code.url .../demo.html?n=black+%26+white&amp;t=horses,small+dogs]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="?n=black+%26+white&t=horses,small+dogs") demo.html?n=black+%26+white&amp;t=horses,small+dogs]

	section.center
		h4 Names work the same in both modes
		p.small Here, in #[code "select"], #[code ?n=brown] shows that chapter and hides every other chapter that has a name.
		p.small In #[code "unhide"], the same #[code ?n=brown] would only reveal a chapter named #[code Brown] that #[code hide] had marked. Nothing else would move.

section(data-name="Groups", data-tag="intro")

	section.center
		h2 Groups
		p Groups are predefined sets of tags and/or names.

	section
		h4 Setting a groups option
		p.small Each group needs a name, and an array of tags and/or an array of names.
		pre
			code.hljs.js(data-line-numbers="3-12").
				Reveal.initialize({
				    //...
				    tagteam: {
				        groups: {
				            "brownpets": {
				                names: ["brown"], tags: ["cats", "dogs"]
				            },
				            "bwhsd": {
				                names: ["black &amp; white"], tags: [ "horses", ["dogs", "small"] ]
				            }
				        }
				    },
				    plugins: [ Tagteam ]
				});
		//-
		p.small Note: in the 2nd group, the tags contain a nested array to be more specific (small dogs only).

	section.center
		h4 Using a #[code g] parameter
		p.small #[code.url .../demo.html?g=brownpets]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="?g=brownpets") demo.html?g=brownpets]

	section
		h4 Setting the #[code mandatorygroup] option
		p.small It is possible to make the use of a group mandatory. This way you avoid the possibility for users to see all slides by just not entering any URL parameter.
		pre
			code.hljs.js(data-line-numbers="4").
				Reveal.initialize({
				    //...
				    tagteam: {
				        mandatorygroup: true,
				        groups: {
				            "brownpets": {
				                names: ["brown"], tags: ["cats", "dogs"]
				            }
				        }
				    },
				    plugins: [ Tagteam ]
				});
		//-
		p.small Note: this is just a simple safeguard. Anyone with a bit of HTML knowledge can find the valid group names.

	section.center
		h4 Using a correct #[code g] parameter
		p.small With #[code mandatorygroup: true] and a correct #[code g] parameter, the slides will show.
		p.small #[code.url .../demo-mandatory.html?g=brownpets]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="demo-mandatory.html?g=brownpets") demo-mandatory.html?g=brownpets]

	section.center
		h4 Using an incorrect #[code g] parameter
		p.small With #[code mandatorygroup: true] and an incorrect #[code g] parameter, the slides will NOT show. Only slides with #[code data-mandatory] will be shown.
		p.small #[code.url .../demo-mandatory.html?g=pinkpets]
		p.small Test it here now: #[a(href="demo-mandatory.html?g=pinkpets") demo-mandatory.html?g=pinkpets]

	section.center
		h4 Groups work the same in both modes
		p.small A group just resolves into tags and names, so it behaves exactly like they do. Here, in #[code "select"], #[code ?g=brownpets] picks what is shown and hides the rest.
		p.small In #[code "unhide"], the same group would only reveal whatever of it #[code hide] had marked. Nothing else would move.

